at discrepancy. The 70B LoRA results are critical for validating the core claim about scale-neutral behavior. I need to map out the planned experiments systematically—WikiEvents runs across Qwen, Llama, and Mistral are already complete, along with the grammar-only and post-hoc filtering variants. Now I'm organizing these into a comprehensive YAML structure grouped by experiment type to lay out detailed predictions for what the results should show. Now I need to write out the YAML configuration. Let me reconsider the 70B LoRA experiments more carefully—I should verify which model is actually being used since the registry entries show training is complete, but I need to confirm whether these are Llama-3.1-70B or a different base model before making predictions. So r16 is the batch identifier for all the 70B experiments, including both the zero-shot and LoRA variants. The LoRA training completed with solid metrics, though the unconstrained evaluation seems to have run without capturing results. Now I need to figure out what R_F1 and DR scores the 70B LoRA will achieve by looking at the patterns from the 8B LoRA results.\n\nLooking at the progression from zero-shot to LoRA across both Qwen and Llama 8B models, I can see consistent improvements of around 35-40 percentage points on SciERC. Since the 70B zero-shot baseline is 11.22%, I'd expect the LoRA version to land somewhere in the 45-50% range. For DocRED, the same models show similar gains, so with a 70B zero-shot baseline of 13.03%, the LoRA should reach somewhere in the 45-50% range as well.\n\nThe dependency relation metrics tell a different story—the 70B LoRA unconstrained should perform much worse than the relation F1 scores, dropping to around 0.5-1.5% on SciERC and 0.3-0.6% on DocRED, while the constrained version should be nearly zero. For entity F1, I'm expecting the 70B LoRA to reach around 68-71% on SciERC and 84-87% on DocRED, which aligns with what I'm seeing from the 8B models.\n\nNow I'm ready to generate the YAML configuration with these predicted performance ranges.\n\nI need to think through which tables and figures are essential for the paper—the main results table covering all models and datasets, the WikiEvents results, ablation studies, and the 70B LoRA breakdown. For visualizations, I'm planning the violation landscape heatmap, per-relation performance breakdown, the architecture diagram, ablation comparisons, and the beam search sweep analysis. I should also make sure I'm capturing the beam search experiments and other critical analyses from the registry that support the best-tier narrative.\n\nWriting YAML output... I need to decide whether to assign new plan IDs to the 70B LoRA experiments or use descriptive names instead, and clarify my approach: I'll include any experiment marked as \"planned\" in the plan (those without a result field), pulling actual data from the registry where available, then organize everything into a comprehensive YAML output covering the WikiEvents experiments and other pending items.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10606,"cache_read_input_tokens":55624,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10606},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b82a3282-ed21-4676-9e91-4d9b245dd612","uuid":"98b5df8e-09fc-4a04-834d-643e5d999bfb"}
